$T0> ##########################################################################
$T0> # PORT EDIT: pin the row set this case actually measures
$T0> #
$T0> # The four counts below are the row set every SELECT in this case is
$T0> # measured against. They are pinned here so that a change in the encoding
$T0> # situation -- a different database character set, a wider or narrower I2,
$T0> # a different client charset -- moves them and turns the case RED at this
$T0> # line, instead of letting it quietly measure a different table than the
$T0> # one its expectation was recorded on.
$T0> #
$T0> # It has already done that job once, and this is the entry it earned.
$T0> # Until 2026-08-21 the counts stood at 8 / 4 / 5 / 3, because three of the
$T0> # fixture INSERTs
$T0> #
$T0> #   INSERT INTO J1 VALUES ( NULL, '<2 hangul>', NULL, 'A1',    0 );
$T0> #   INSERT INTO J1 VALUES ( 3E+6, '<2 hangul>', NULL, 'A2', NULL );
$T0> #   INSERT INTO J2 VALUES (    1, '<2 hangul>', '30-JUN-2003', 'A1', -1.2 );
$T0> #
$T0> # were failing with [ERR-21069 : Invalid data type length : I2.]. I2 is
$T0> # VARCHAR(5) and the literals are two Hangul characters -- four bytes in
$T0> # the EUC-KR body of this file, six once a client conversion to UTF8 gets
$T0> # in the way. That expectation had been recorded on the isolated instance
$T0> # scripts/global-index/testdb.sh built with `server create UTF8 UTF8`,
$T0> # which is not the instance the runner uses. On the standard one -- client
$T0> # NLS_USE and database character set both KO16KSC5601 -- there is no
$T0> # conversion, the three rows land, and the counts are the fixture's own
$T0> # 10 / 5 / 5 / 3. That is also what the original qp4 oracle records
$T0> # (JoinTest_A4_64.lst: 50 rows selected, no ERR-21069 anywhere). The pin
$T0> # is back on the original's value.
$T0> #
$T0> # The fixture inserts 10 / 5 / 5 / 3 rows; this port measures the same.
$T0> ##########################################################################

